feat(realtime): MCP->API broadcast webhook + cross-process SignalR

Until now, the MCP server's mutating tools updated the database
silently. The SignalR broadcaster in the API only fired on the
API's own Wolverine domain-event handlers, so a Web client
that had joined a board saw nothing when an AI moved a card.

What changed:

- New endpoint POST /api/internal/broadcast on the API. Auth
  is a shared secret in the X-Internal-Secret header
  (Internal:Secret config). The body takes an optional
  boardId, listId, or cardId (the API resolves the board via
  the same EF Core model; streaming with AsAsyncEnumerable
  because the strongly-typed-id access path can't be
  translated), the SignalR method name, and the typed payload.
  Method-name dispatch drives the matching IBoardClient call
  on the board:{boardId} SignalR group. Unknown method -> 400;
  missing secret config -> 503; wrong secret -> 401.

- Shared payload records moved from
  src/Cardscape.Api/Hubs/IBoardClient.cs to
  src/Cardscape.Application/Realtime/BoardEventPayloads.cs so
  the MCP project can serialise the same shape to the
  broadcast endpoint without duplicating records. The API
  hub keeps the same method signatures; the records just
  live one layer down.

- New IBoardPushClient in Cardscape.Mcp/Realtime with typed
  methods (PushCardCreatedAsync, PushCardMovedAsync, ...)
  implemented by HttpBoardPushClient. The HTTP client
  targets the API base URL (Cardscape:ApiBaseUrl) and
  forwards the shared secret. Broadcasts are best-effort:
  a warning is logged on failure but the tool result is
  already committed in the database.

- The five mutating MCP tools (lists_create, cards_create,
  cards_move, cards_complete, cards_reopen, comments_add)
  now call the push client after success. cards_move looks
  up the source list id via ICardRepository before invoking
  the command so the FromListId field of the payload is
  correct.

- appsettings.json gains a commented-out Internal:Secret
  placeholder so the required production config is
  visible in the diff.

Tests: 5 new integration tests on the broadcast endpoint
(unconfigured / wrong secret / unknown method / happy path
with boardId / listId path that requires the database to
resolve). 187 unit + 25 integration = 212 green.

The Web client doesn't change: it already joins board:{id}
on BoardDetail.razor and listens to the same IBoardClient
events the broadcaster emits, so an AI-driven edit now shows
up there in real time.
